questions.description:
An LRU cache with **capacity 3** runs this sequence. Using a key moves it to the most-recently-used end.

```js
put('A', 1);
put('B', 2);
put('C', 3);
get('A');
put('D', 4); // capacity exceeded — one key is evicted
```

A frequently called function caches its results in a module-level `Map`, keyed by a unique per-request id. Nothing is ever deleted:

```js
const cache = new Map();
function getReport(reqId, build) {
  if (!cache.has(reqId)) cache.set(reqId, build());
  return cache.get(reqId);
}
```

questions.explanation:
A JS `Map` remembers **insertion order** and lets you read it cheaply, which gets you most of an LRU cache for free:

```js
function createLRU(max = 100) {
  const cache = new Map();
  return {
    get(key) {
      if (!cache.has(key)) return undefined;
      const v = cache.get(key);
      cache.delete(key);   // re-insert to move to the MRU end
      cache.set(key, v);
      return v;
    },
    set(key, v) {
      cache.delete(key);             // refresh position if already present
      cache.set(key, v);
      if (cache.size > max) {
        cache.delete(cache.keys().next().value); // evict the LRU (oldest) key
      }
    },
  };
}
```

What the options test: `set` on an **existing** key keeps its original position, so you `delete` then `set` to move it to the end. `keys().next().value` gives you the least recently used key. And there's **no** automatic cap, so the size check and eviction are up to you. A bounded cache like this one fixes the unbounded memo `Map` leak.
